    "https://georgetown.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Georgetown_Admin_Careers/job/Main-Campus/Director-of-Research-and-Learning-Partnerships-for-the-Collaborative-on-Global-Children-s-Issues--Walsh-School-of-Foreign-Service--SFS-_JR27726": {
      "title": "Director of Research and Learning Partnerships for the Collaborative on Global Children's Issues, Walsh School of Foreign Service (SFS)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-07\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nGeorgetown University comprises two unique campuses in the nation’s capital. With the Hilltop Campus located in the heart of the historic Georgetown neighborhood, and the Capitol Campus, just minutes from the U.S. Capitol and U.S. Supreme Court, Georgetown University offers rigorous academic programs, a global perspective, and unparalleled opportunities to engage with Washington, D.C. Our community is a close-knit group of remarkable individuals driven by intellectual inquiry, a commitment to social justice, and a shared dedication to making a difference in the world. Requirements Job Overview The Collaborative on Global Children’s Issues fosters cross-disciplinary research and dialogue on critical and emerging issues affecting children and youth worldwide. We convene researchers, policymakers, practitioners, faith leaders, and young people to promote evidence-based, innovative, and solutions-oriented approaches to support children and youth in situations of adversity.    Values: Committed to creating opportunities that center children and youth; Grounded in the lived experiences of children and youth, their families, and communities; Responsive to current needs and useful to actors working in a variety of contexts and capacities to meet them; Evidence-informed and solutions-oriented; and Building effective bridges between young people and stakeholders involved in practice, policy, and research.   Approach: Convene: Facilitate interdisciplinary and intergenerational engagement and collaboration on critical and emerging issues affecting children and youth around the world.  Facilitate research, policy analysis, and implementation science: Strengthen our field by bridging gaps between research, policy, practice, and the lived experience of children and youth in situations of adversity.  Learn from the front line: Partner with innovators working at community, national, regional, and global levels.  Train: Equip the next generation of global leaders to prioritize the experiences, well-being, and agency of children and youth around the world by building curricular and career pathways that promote child- and youth-centered approaches in research, policy, and practice.    The Director of Research and Learning Partnerships (the Director) will help lead the Collaborative on Global Children’s Issues and support the Executive Director (ED) in fostering cross-disciplinary research and dialogue on critical and emerging issues affecting children and youth worldwide, with a particular focus on children in adversity and pathways to resilience. The Director will work closely with the ED to build strategic partnerships; advance research and educational initiatives; develop high-impact events and learning opportunities; and support thought-leadership that brings together policy, practice, research, and the lived experiences of children and young people. The Director will help increase awareness and recognition of the Collaborative as an incubator of ideas, conversations, and leadership to promote evidence-based, innovative, and solutions-oriented approaches to support children and youth in situations of adversity.    Vision and Strategic Leadership : The Director of Research and Learning Partnerships will help shape and implement the Collaborative’s strategy, developing activities that advance the Collaborative’s mission, values, and impact. Specifically, the Director will oversee the development and implementation of a multidisciplinary research and learning agenda and build research and learning partnerships with faculty and staff from other Georgetown University programs and departments, as well as external stakeholders.   Academic and Research Program Development : The Director of Research and Learning Partnerships will advance curricular content on children and youth at Georgetown, and increase opportunities for student and faculty engagement and learning. The Director will advance the Collaborative’s efforts to offer university-wide credit and non-credit learning opportunities for graduate students, undergraduate students, and other stakeholders. In addition, the Director will work to advance a new Minor or Certificate Program in Children’s Issues.  The Director will also support implementation and further development of a Student Fellows Program and Global Fellows Program, and help the Collaborative bring in scholar-practitioners whose research interests and profiles contribute to the Collaborative’s mission. The Director will also provide overarching management, quality assurance, and ensure the strategic coherence of papers and reports published by the Collaborative, contributing to sector-wide knowledge sharing and learning.   Fundraising and Donor Engagement : The Director of Research and Learning Partnerships will maintain and build relationships with current and prospective donors.  The Director will support donor prospecting, grant writing, donor engagement, and reporting, in collaboration with the Executive Director.   Work Interactions The Director of Research and Learning Partnerships reports to the Collaborative’s Executive Director and works closely with the Program Manager.   Requirements and Qualifications A bachelor’s degree and a minimum of 10 years of research and program management experience in child protection, child development, child rights, social services, or a related field. Demonstrated inclusive leadership skills and a track record of building partnerships that advance the well-being of children, youth, and families globally. Experience designing, leading, or contributing to multidisciplinary research agendas, including overseeing the development of papers, reports, or other research products. Strong written and oral communication skills, including experience writing for academic, policy, and general audiences. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple projects in a complex, multi-faceted environment. Experience working with vendors and partners from a variety of international backgrounds. Proven skills in time management, organization, and communication. Ability to exercise discretion and maintain composure under pressure. Proactive, team-spirited disposition, with the ability to consult with others and make independent decisions. Collaborative work style, with openness to feedback and adaptability to evolving priorities.   Preferred: Doctoral degree in a relevant field (e.g., public policy, international development, psychology, social work, or education). Experience in a higher-education setting, including curriculum development, teaching, or managing student fellowship or mentorship programs. Experience with donor engagement, grant writing, or fundraising, ideally in an academic or nonprofit context. Familiarity with Georgetown University or similar academic institutions' research and administrative structures. Publication record or other demonstrated contributions to the field of children's issues, child rights, or global child welfare.   Work Mode Designation This position has been designated as Hybrid. Please note that work mode designations are regularly reviewed in order to meet the evolving needs of the University.  Such review may necessitate a change to a position’s mode of work designation. Complete details about Georgetown University’s mode of work designations for staff positions can be found on the Department of Human Resources website: https://hr.georgetown.edu/mode-of-work-designation . Pay Range: The projected salary or hourly pay range for this position which represents the full range of anticipated compensation is: $80,429.00 - $157,238.93 Compensation is determined by a number of factors including, but not limited to, the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, skills, and certifications, as well as the University’s business needs and external factors. Current Georgetown Employees: If you currently work at Georgetown University, please exit this website and login to GMS ( gms.georgetown.edu ) using your Net ID and password. Then select the Career worklet on your GMS Home dashboard to view Jobs at Georgetown. Submission Guidelines: Please note that in order to be considered an applicant for any position at Georgetown University you must submit a resume for each position of interest for which you believe you are qualified. Documents are not kept on file for future positions. Need Assistance: If you are a qualified individual with a disability and need a reasonable accommodations for any part of the application and hiring process, please visit the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance website for general information about requesting accommodations , as well as information about requesting accommodations specifically for applicants . You also can also contact the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance at (202) 687-4798 or oeoc@georgetown.edu . Need some assistance with the application process? Please call 202-687-2500. For more information about the suite of benefits, professional development and community involvement opportunities that make up Georgetown's commitment to its employees, please visit the Georgetown Works website . EEO Statement: GU is an Equal Opportunity Employer . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply, and will receive consideration for employment without regard to age, citizenship, color, disability, family responsibilities, gender identity and expression, genetic information, marital status, matriculation, national origin, race, religion, personal appearance, political affiliation, sex, sexual orientation, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law . Benefits: Georgetown University offers a comprehensive and competitive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, disability and life insurance, retirement savings, tuition assistance, work-life balance benefits, employee discounts and an array of voluntary insurance options. You can learn more about benefits and eligibility on the Department of Human Resources website ."
    },
    "https://georgetown.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Georgetown_Admin_Careers/job/Medical-Center/Research-Technician_JR27742": {
      "title": "Research Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-07\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nGeorgetown University comprises two unique campuses in the nation’s capital. With the Hilltop Campus located in the heart of the historic Georgetown neighborhood, and the Capitol Campus, just minutes from the U.S. Capitol and U.S. Supreme Court, Georgetown University offers rigorous academic programs, a global perspective, and unparalleled opportunities to engage with Washington, D.C. Our community is a close-knit group of remarkable individuals driven by intellectual inquiry, a commitment to social justice, and a shared dedication to making a difference in the world. Requirements Job Overview The Research Technician supports NIH-funded research in the laboratory of Dr. Patrick Forcelli, which studies the brain circuits that control seizures and the long-term effects of early-life exposure to anti-seizure and psychiatric medications on brain development and behavior. The technician carries out in vivo experiments in rodents, performs histological processing and microscopy, manages and analyzes experimental data, and keeps the laboratory, animal colony, and regulatory records running smoothly so that the lab’s research projects progress on schedule.   Additional duties, include but are not limited to: Conducting in vivo rodent experiments, including drug administration, behavioral testing, stereotaxic surgery, and EEG/seizure monitoring. Processing brain tissue for histology and immunohistochemistry, and acquiring and quantifying microscope images. Organizing, curating, and analyzing experimental data (behavior, EEG, histology) and preparing summaries for the PI and lab members. Managing breeding colonies and maintaining animal-use records in compliance with IACUC protocols. Ordering supplies, reconciling charges, maintaining equipment, and supporting general laboratory operations.   Work Interactions The Research Technician works within the Forcelli Laboratory in the Department of Pharmacology & Physiology and reports directly to the Principal Investigator, Dr. Patrick Forcelli, Professor and Chair. The technician works day to day with the lab’s postdoctoral fellows, Ph.D. students, and undergraduate researchers, and interacts with Department of Comparative Medicine (vivarium) staff, core facilities, and departmental administrative staff for purchasing and grant administration. This position has no direct reports. By enabling rigorous, reproducible research on epilepsy and developmental neuropharmacology, the position advances Georgetown University’s mission of research, education, and service to improve human health.   Requirements and Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in neuroscience, biology, psychology, pharmacology, or a related field, and at least one year of hands-on laboratory research experience (undergraduate research experience qualifies) Must be able to work with live animals (rodents), follow detailed written protocols, and keep accurate records.   Preferred: Experience with rodent handling, dosing, behavioral testing, or stereotaxic surgery; experience with histology, immunohistochemistry, and fluorescence microscopy; familiarity with EEG or electrophysiological data; working knowledge of data analysis software (e.g., GraphPad Prism, ImageJ/FIJI, MATLAB, Python, or R); and prior completion of IACUC/CITI animal research training.   Work Mode Designation This position has been designated as On-Campus. Please note that work mode designations are regularly reviewed in order to meet the evolving needs of the University.  Such review may necessitate a change to a position’s mode of work designation. Complete details about Georgetown University’s mode of work designations for staff positions can be found on the Department of Human Resources website: https://hr.georgetown.edu/mode-of-work-designation . Pay Range: The projected salary or hourly pay range for this position which represents the full range of anticipated compensation is: $18.40 - $27.09 Compensation is determined by a number of factors including, but not limited to, the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, skills, and certifications, as well as the University’s business needs and external factors. Current Georgetown Employees: If you currently work at Georgetown University, please exit this website and login to GMS ( gms.georgetown.edu ) using your Net ID and password. Then select the Career worklet on your GMS Home dashboard to view Jobs at Georgetown. Submission Guidelines: Please note that in order to be considered an applicant for any position at Georgetown University you must submit a resume for each position of interest for which you believe you are qualified. Documents are not kept on file for future positions. Need Assistance: If you are a qualified individual with a disability and need a reasonable accommodations for any part of the application and hiring process, please visit the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance website for general information about requesting accommodations , as well as information about requesting accommodations specifically for applicants . You also can also contact the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance at (202) 687-4798 or oeoc@georgetown.edu . Need some assistance with the application process? Please call 202-687-2500. For more information about the suite of benefits, professional development and community involvement opportunities that make up Georgetown's commitment to its employees, please visit the Georgetown Works website . EEO Statement: GU is an Equal Opportunity Employer . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply, and will receive consideration for employment without regard to age, citizenship, color, disability, family responsibilities, gender identity and expression, genetic information, marital status, matriculation, national origin, race, religion, personal appearance, political affiliation, sex, sexual orientation, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law . Benefits: Georgetown University offers a comprehensive and competitive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, disability and life insurance, retirement savings, tuition assistance, work-life balance benefits, employee discounts and an array of voluntary insurance options. You can learn more about benefits and eligibility on the Department of Human Resources website ."
    },
    "https://georgetown.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Georgetown_Admin_Careers/job/Pre-Clinical-Science-Lower-Level/Clinical-Research-Coordinator-2_JR27759": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Coordinator 2",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-07\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nGeorgetown University comprises two unique campuses in the nation’s capital. With the Hilltop Campus located in the heart of the historic Georgetown neighborhood, and the Capitol Campus, just minutes from the U.S. Capitol and U.S. Supreme Court, Georgetown University offers rigorous academic programs, a global perspective, and unparalleled opportunities to engage with Washington, D.C. Our community is a close-knit group of remarkable individuals driven by intellectual inquiry, a commitment to social justice, and a shared dedication to making a difference in the world. Requirements Job Overview Serves as Clinical Research Coordinator 2 for the Cellular Therapy clinical trials conducted through the Lombardi Clinical Trials Office (Lombardi CTO) of the Lombardi Comprehensive Cancer Center at Georgetown University. Additional duties include, but are not limited to: Serving as Cellular Therapy champion liaise with Lombardi CTO, MedStar Health, and GUMC Departments, regarding the status of studies in the start-up process to support timely activation of new Cellular Therapy trials and provide regular updates for activated trials Establish and maintain regular meeting cadence for Cellular Therapy clinical trials Collaborate with Cellular Therapy Co-Leads to create, establish, and distribute meeting agendas and document/distribute minutes and actions items from each meeting Schedule in-service training for appropriate staff assigned on clinical trials  Responsible for overall coordination of assigned clinical trials throughout the trial lifecycle. Interface with study participants and obtain and document informed consent according to protocol, institutional policies, and regulatory guidelines. Schedule and coordinate study-related procedures and assessments while maintaining protocol integrity and patient safety and in accordance with Good Clinical Practice (GCP) guidelines. Coordinate collection of research specimens per protocol requirements Regularly update the Clinical Trials Management System (CTMS) with patient information and key dates to support responsible financial management of clinical trials. Collaborate with Data Coordinators(s) to ensure documentation for the clinical trial is complete and accurate in compliance with protocol and sponsor requirements; assist with query resolution in a timely manner. Schedule/facilitate sponsor meetings including PSV, SIV, MV Document and report serious adverse events per protocol and institutional policy, with input from PI or senior member of clinical research team Act as a liaison with hospital staff, physicians, IRB, review committees, clinical research finance team, and auditors Conduct follow-up with study participants in accordance with established standards and protocols. Act as a liaison with sponsors during study start-up activities and participate in site initiation visits, monitoring visits, and audits. Identify potential barriers to patient and physician participation in clinical trials; develop and implement methods to alleviate those barriers. Actively participate in meetings and committees, as assigned. Other duties as assigned.   The Clinical Research Coordinator 2 is expected to perform all duties in accordance with applicable laws and regulations and adhere to Georgetown University Medical Center’s philosophies, policies, and SOPs, as well as work in compliance with Human Subject Protection, patient confidentiality/HIPAA, and GCP.   Work Interactions Clinical Operations Team Manager Disease Group members: Principal Investigators, Physicians, Clinicians Lombardi Clinical Trials Office (CTO) members: Clinical Research Nurses, Clinical Research Coordinators, Data Coordinators, Regulatory Coordinators, Laboratory Technicians External department clinicians and staff: Infusion Nurses, Oncology Pharmacists, hospital staff Clinical trial sponsors, auditors, and study monitors Minimum Requirements and Qualifications Bachelor’s Degree in a scientific or health related field required. Three (3) to five (5) years of related experience in clinical research preferred. Excellent project coordination skills, verbal and written communication skills, organizational skills, attention to detail Ability to work independently and function within a team Knowledge of clinical research, research processes, and Good Clinical Practice (GCP) Reliable and able to prioritize competing responsibilities. Work Mode Designation This position has been designated as On-Campus. Please note that work mode designations are regularly reviewed in order to meet the evolving needs of the University.  Such review may necessitate a change to a position’s mode of work designation. Complete details about Georgetown University’s mode of work designations for staff positions can be found on the Department of Human Resources website: https://hr.georgetown.edu/mode-of-work-designation .   Pay Range: The projected salary or hourly pay range for this position which represents the full range of anticipated compensation is: $44,022.00 - $73,406.80 Compensation is determined by a number of factors including, but not limited to, the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, skills, and certifications, as well as the University’s business needs and external factors. Current Georgetown Employees: If you currently work at Georgetown University, please exit this website and login to GMS ( gms.georgetown.edu ) using your Net ID and password. Then select the Career worklet on your GMS Home dashboard to view Jobs at Georgetown. Submission Guidelines: Please note that in order to be considered an applicant for any position at Georgetown University you must submit a resume for each position of interest for which you believe you are qualified. Documents are not kept on file for future positions. Need Assistance: If you are a qualified individual with a disability and need a reasonable accommodations for any part of the application and hiring process, please visit the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance website for general information about requesting accommodations , as well as information about requesting accommodations specifically for applicants . You also can also contact the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance at (202) 687-4798 or oeoc@georgetown.edu . Need some assistance with the application process? Please call 202-687-2500. For more information about the suite of benefits, professional development and community involvement opportunities that make up Georgetown's commitment to its employees, please visit the Georgetown Works website . EEO Statement: GU is an Equal Opportunity Employer . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply, and will receive consideration for employment without regard to age, citizenship, color, disability, family responsibilities, gender identity and expression, genetic information, marital status, matriculation, national origin, race, religion, personal appearance, political affiliation, sex, sexual orientation, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law . Benefits: Georgetown University offers a comprehensive and competitive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, disability and life insurance, retirement savings, tuition assistance, work-life balance benefits, employee discounts and an array of voluntary insurance options. You can learn more about benefits and eligibility on the Department of Human Resources website ."
    },
    "https://georgetown.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Georgetown_Admin_Careers/job/1000-New-Jersey-Ave-1st-Floor/Patient-Navigator_JR27760": {
      "title": "Patient Navigator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-07\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nGeorgetown University comprises two unique campuses in the nation’s capital. With the Hilltop Campus located in the heart of the historic Georgetown neighborhood, and the Capitol Campus, just minutes from the U.S. Capitol and U.S. Supreme Court, Georgetown University offers rigorous academic programs, a global perspective, and unparalleled opportunities to engage with Washington, D.C. Our community is a close-knit group of remarkable individuals driven by intellectual inquiry, a commitment to social justice, and a shared dedication to making a difference in the world. Requirements Job Overivew The primary function of this position is to serve as a senior bilingual patient navigator at the Ralph Lauren Center for Cancer Prevention, Lombardi Comprehensive Cancer Center. The scope of the position includes conducting community outreach and education; assisting patients with access to breast, cervical, colon, and lung cancer screening services; building and maintaining relationships with community partners/service providers; and tracking interventions and outcomes. The senior patient navigator will also create evidence-based navigation workflows, update existing navigation protocols to align with national guidelines, train other navigation team members on best practices, and supervise the collection of navigation metrics. In addition, the navigator will work with center leadership to design and offer additional services relevant to cancer prevention and survivorship. The senior patient navigator partners with staff members, clinical staff, and leadership to refine existing systems that proactively identify and address patient needs. Essential skills include thorough attention to detail, strong organizational skills, effective interpersonal skills, strong verbal and written communication skills, and the ability to train others. Some evening and weekend hours are required.   This position will also support efforts of the Ralph Lauren Center for Cancer Prevention with regards to research studies applicable to navigated patients. Provide breast health education, identify barriers to screening, reduce known barriers, facilitate appointments, conduct eligibility screening and ensure patient compliance with recommended breast, cervical, colon, and lung cancer screenings.    Additional duties include, but are not limited to: Navigate patients to cancer screening, diagnostic and treatment services Proactive outreach to community partners and primary care practices to identify new collaborators Creating revised navigation workflows and metrics that align with evidence-based guidelines Train new navigation team members and provide expertise on metrics collection and reporting Work closely with center leadership to identify and design new services relevant to cancer prevention and survivorship Work with data manger to collate and report navigation-related metrics for internal and external reports   Work Interactions This position reports to the Director of Cancer Screening and works closely with all of the center's staff and faculty.   Requirements and Qualifications At least Bachelor’s degree in health-related field is required. At least 5-years’ experience working with patient navigation and community engagement. More experience is preferable. Proficiency in conversational English and Spanish.  Must have flexibility to work at least one weekend a month. Technical Qualifications or Specialized Certification: Up-to-date knowledge of patient navigation gold standards, social media advertising, and metrics tracking systems   Work Mode Designation This position has been designated as On-Campus. Please note that work mode designations are regularly reviewed in order to meet the evolving needs of the University.  Such review may necessitate a change to a position’s mode of work designation. Complete details about Georgetown University’s mode of work designations for staff positions can be found on the Department of Human Resources website: https://hr.georgetown.edu/mode-of-work-designation . Pay Range: The projected salary or hourly pay range for this position which represents the full range of anticipated compensation is: $44,022.00 - $73,406.80 Compensation is determined by a number of factors including, but not limited to, the candidate’s individual qualifications, experience, education, skills, and certifications, as well as the University’s business needs and external factors. Current Georgetown Employees: If you currently work at Georgetown University, please exit this website and login to GMS ( gms.georgetown.edu ) using your Net ID and password. Then select the Career worklet on your GMS Home dashboard to view Jobs at Georgetown. Submission Guidelines: Please note that in order to be considered an applicant for any position at Georgetown University you must submit a resume for each position of interest for which you believe you are qualified. Documents are not kept on file for future positions. Need Assistance: If you are a qualified individual with a disability and need a reasonable accommodations for any part of the application and hiring process, please visit the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance website for general information about requesting accommodations , as well as information about requesting accommodations specifically for applicants . You also can also contact the Office for Equal Opportunity Compliance at (202) 687-4798 or oeoc@georgetown.edu . Need some assistance with the application process? Please call 202-687-2500. For more information about the suite of benefits, professional development and community involvement opportunities that make up Georgetown's commitment to its employees, please visit the Georgetown Works website . EEO Statement: GU is an Equal Opportunity Employer . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply, and will receive consideration for employment without regard to age, citizenship, color, disability, family responsibilities, gender identity and expression, genetic information, marital status, matriculation, national origin, race, religion, personal appearance, political affiliation, sex, sexual orientation, veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law . Benefits: Georgetown University offers a comprehensive and competitive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, disability and life insurance, retirement savings, tuition assistance, work-life balance benefits, employee discounts and an array of voluntary insurance options. You can learn more about benefits and eligibility on the Department of Human Resources website ."
